e_Organics_and_Biomonomers_Identified_in_HCN_Polymers_An_Overview)\n  18. Crossroads at the Origin of Prebiotic Chemical Complexity: Hydrogen Cyanide Product Diversification (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jpca.3c01504)\n  19. Marta Ruiz-bermejo - Profile on Academia.edu (https://independent.academia.edu/MartaRuizbermejo)\n  20. Which Amino Acids Should Be Used in Prebiotic Chemistry Studies? (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-008-9150-5)\n  21. (PDF) The Miller Volcanic Spark Discharge Experiment (https://www.academia.edu/121732208/The_Miller_Volcanic_Spark_Discharge_Experiment)\n  22. Analysis of Stanley Lloyd Miller's Experiment (https://www.ukessays.com/essays/biology/analysis-stanley-lloyd-millers-9684.php)\n  23. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/274088015_Common_origins_of_RNA_protein_and_lipid_precursors_in_a_cyanosulfidic_protometabolism)\n  24. Energetics of Amino Acid Synthesis in Hydrothermal Ecosystems (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.281.5383.1659)\n  25. Structure of imidazole and imidazole-derivatives with atom... (https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Structure-of-imidazole-and-imidazole-derivatives-with-atom-numbering-The-unsubstituted_fig1_324783638)\n  26. A thermodynamic basis for prebiotic amino acid synthesis and the nature of the first genetic code - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19566427/?dopt=Abstract)\n  27. MetaCyc imidazole-lactate degradation (http://vm-trypanocyc.toulouse.inra.fr/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&object=PWY-5029)\n  28. Which Amino Acids Should Be Used in Prebiotic Chemistry Studies? (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/23390356_Which_Amino_Acids_Should_Be_Used_in_Prebiotic_Chemistry_Studies)\n  29. Histidine Self-assembly and Stability on Mineral Surfaces as a Model of Prebiotic Chemical Evolution: An Experimental and Computational Approach (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-021-09606-3)\n  30. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  31. The science from asteroid sample return missions (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.22182)\n  32. Life's Building Blocks Found in Bennu Samples - Eos.org (https://eos.org/articles/lifes-building-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  33. Prebiotically Plausible Peptides can Self-assemble into \u03b2-rich Nanostructures (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.11.09.687475.full.pdf)\n  34. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n  35. Evolution of complex chemical mixtures reveals combinatorial compression and population synchronicity (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-025-01734-x)\n  36. Synthetic connectivity, emergence, and self-regeneration in the network of prebiotic chemistry (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aaw1955)\n  37. A Plausible Route to a Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Histidine N. Aylward , (https://www.wseas.org/multimedia/journals/biology/2012/54-084.pdf)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Histidine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Histidine is treated as a late, low-flux, contested product. The network is centered on the only experimentally demonstrated small-molecule framework: surface formose chemistry supplies aldotetroses from formaldehyde, HCN/NH3 concentration supplies the contested amidine formamidine, tetrose-amidine annulation yields the decisive low-yield imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and HCN/NH3 then close the route by Strecker chemistry. Imidazole-4-glycol and imidazole-4-ethanol are included as experimentally observed side branches and speculative salvage reservoirs. The 10 listed pathways are route variants within one connected network, not 10 equally validated syntheses. Following the supplied evidence, hydrothermal, cyanosulfidic, meteoritic, trace HCN-polymer, spark-discharge, and semi-biotic PRPP/ATP proposals are not promoted to productive edges here because they lack a comparably resolved small-molecule reaction sequence.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrocyanic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"erythrose\",\n      \"common_name\": \"erythrose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"threose\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanimidamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formamidine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethane-1,2-diol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethan-1-ol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-ethanol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose initiation: formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline wet-dry formose regime in evaporitic pools or tidal flats; anoxic surface; low steady-state glycolaldehyde expected\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net formaldehyde dimerization via enediol/aldol chemistry in the formose network.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The histidine papers assume a formose origin for the C4 sugar pool; this compressed edge represents the initiation of that upstream chemistry. It is not a histidine-specific experiment in the supplied report, and the main caveat is the low, unstable sugar inventory emphasized by later critiques (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose homologation: glycolaldehyde to glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline wet-dry formose regime; compressed representation of the triose-forming segment of the formose network\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol homologation followed by enolization and isomerization within the formose network.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is included because the Shen route requires upstream conversion of formaldehyde to a C4 sugar precursor. As with rxn_001, it is a compressed, non-histidine-specific upstream edge whose relevance is limited by the poor prebiotic accumulation of higher sugars noted in the critique literature (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose homologation: glyceraldehyde to aldotetroses\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline surface pool; aldotetrose mixture expected; erythrose and threose remain minor and unstable\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Further aldol homologation gives an aldotetrose mixture, here represented by erythrose and threose epimers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step generates the C4 aldoses needed for imidazole side-chain construction. Shen et al. used erythrose as the key sugar, while later critiques stressed that erythrose and threose are minor, unstable formose products and therefore difficult to accumulate prebiotically (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Amidine supply: HCN and ammonia to formamidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN/NH3-rich evaporitic concentration; transient amidine pool; rapid hydrolysis competes\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net ammonolysis and addition to cyanide-derived imidoyl species giving methanimidamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formamidine is the required amidine feedstock in the Shen-Oro route, but the supplied literature does not show a robust geochemical accumulation route at the needed concentration. Later critiques emphasize that about 0.3 M formamidine is hard to justify because formamidine hydrolyzes rapidly; this edge is therefore necessary for the network but strongly contested geochemically (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine annulation to imidazole aldehyde/glycol pair\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated aqueous pool; exact pH and time from the original report were not recovered; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbonyl condensation of the aldotetrose with formamidine, Amadori-type rearrangement, and cyclodehydration to the imidazole ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the central experimentally reported bottleneck for abiotic histidine chemistry. Shen, Yang, Miller and Oro obtained imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (up to 1.6% relative to erythrose) together with imidazole-4-glycol from erythrose plus formamidine in water, without a mineral catalyst; uncatalyzed imidazole-forming chemistry is also consistent with broader imidazole condensations (Shen et al., 1987; Oro et al., 1984). Its prebiotic significance is heavily challenged because both erythrose supply and formamidine persistence are poor (Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-formamidine annulation to imidazole aldehyde/glycol pair\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated aqueous pool; evidence-guided extrapolation from the erythrose experiment; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Same tetrose-formamidine annulation manifold as rxn_005, with loss of tetrose stereochemical information during ring closure.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The provided experiments centered on erythrose, not isolated threose. This edge is included as an evidence-guided extrapolation because Vazquez-Salazar et al. note that the stereocenters of the tetrose are lost during annulation and therefore any C4 aldose should in principle serve; the reaction is chemically plausible but remains unverified for threose specifically (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia annulation to reduced imidazole pair\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous erythrose/H2CO/NH3 mixture; exact pH and time from the original report were not recovered; reduced side-chain branch\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-mediated carbonyl condensation and imidazole ring closure produce reduced C2 side-chain imidazole derivatives.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. reported that erythrose, formaldehyde and ammonia give imidazole-4-ethanol (5.4%) and imidazole-4-glycol (6.8%). This branch is experimentally supported as a side-product source, but it is not by itself a histidine route because conversion of these reduced products to the required aldehyde was only proposed, not demonstrated (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative salvage: imidazole-4-glycol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"UV-exposed drying film or geothermal cycling; net dehydration/oxidation; explicitly undemonstrated\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net redox and rearrangement of the hydroxy-substituted C2 side chain to the aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. argued that imidazole-4-glycol could, in principle, be converted by other prebiotic reactions to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, but no such transformation was shown in the supplied literature. This node is therefore explicitly speculative and encodes the open redox bottleneck highlighted by later critiques (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative salvage: imidazole-4-ethanol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"UV-exposed drying film or geothermal cycling; net oxidation step; explicitly undemonstrated\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net oxidation of the primary alcohol side chain to the aldehyde during UV and thermal cycling.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The 1987 study proposed, but did not demonstrate, that imidazole-4-ethanol could be prebiotically oxidized to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde. This edge is thus speculative, included only because the reduced alcohol was experimentally observed and the literature explicitly identifies conversion to the aldehyde as the missing step (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN/NH3-rich pool; mechanistic decomposition of the overall 1990 one-pot result\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine or iminium with the aldehyde, cyanide adds, and the aminonitrile is trapped.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. 1990 demonstrated the overall conversion of the unpurified imidazole-4-acetaldehyde-containing mixture to histidine after addition of HCN and ammonia. The aminonitrile itself was not isolated in the supplied sources, so this step is a mechanistic decomposition of the validated overall transformation, supported by general Strecker chemistry and by pairing of histidine with imidazole-4-acetaldehyde in Strecker models (Shen et al., 1990; Cobb et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged aqueous heating or geothermal cycling in prebiotic pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis to the amino acid carboxylate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is also mechanistically inferred rather than individually reported for histidine in the supplied literature. It is nevertheless the standard completion of a Strecker sequence; the main unresolved issue is selectivity because competing cyanohydrin and hydroxy-acid channels were not quantified for the imidazole aldehyde system (Shen et al., 1990; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018; Cobb et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker-hydrolysis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN and NH3 added directly to the unpurified aldehyde-forming mixture; exact pH and time were not recovered from the supplied report\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net Strecker sequence from aldehyde to aminonitrile followed by in situ hydrolysis in the same reaction system.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the only experimentally demonstrated small-molecule closure to histidine in the supplied literature. Shen, Yang, Miller and Oro identified histidine by LC, HPLC, thermospray LC-MS, and tandem MS after adding HCN and ammonia to the imidazole-4-acetaldehyde-forming mixture, with an overall yield of about 3.5% relative to erythrose. The chemistry is sound, but its prebiotic status remains contested because the route depends on unstable tetroses and concentrated formamidine, and no independent modern isotope-labeled replication is cited in the provided review (Shen et al., 1990; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Evidence-anchored erythrose one-pot route\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde-derived erythrose and HCN/NH3-derived formamidine converge at the experimentally anchored annulation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, followed by the one-pot Strecker closure.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose direct stepwise Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Same erythrose annulation entry as P1, but the terminal one-pot closure is decomposed into aminonitrile formation and later hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose glycol-salvage one-pot route\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the experimentally observed imidazole-4-glycol coproduct from the erythrose/formamidine reaction as a speculative reservoir that is converted to the aldehyde before one-pot Strecker closure.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose glycol-salvage stepwise route\",\n      \"description\": \"Same as P3 but with explicit aminonitrile formation and later hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose ethanol-salvage one-pot route\",\n      \"description\": \"Starts from the experimentally observed erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia branch to imidazole-4-ethanol, then invokes a speculative oxidation to the aldehyde before one-pot Strecker closure.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose ethanol-salvage stepwise route\",\n      \"description\": \"Alcohol-reservoir salvage variant of P5 with explicit aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose glycol-reservoir route from the H2CO/NH3 branch\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the glycol coproduct generated in the erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia branch and converts it speculatively to the aldehyde before one-pot Strecker closure.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose extrapolated one-pot route\",\n      \"description\": \"Evidence-guided extrapolation in which the aldotetrose epimer threose replaces erythrose in the formamidine annulation; final closure uses the experimentally demonstrated one-pot Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose extrapolated stepwise route\",\n      \"description\": \"Threose-based extrapolation of the Shen pathway with explicit aminonitrile and hydrolysis substeps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose glycol-salvage stepwise route\",\n      \"description\": \"Threose-based pathway that passes through the coproduct imidazole-4-glycol before speculative salvage to the aldehyde and stepwise Strecker completion.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine", "overall_strategy": "Histidine is treated as a late, low-flux, contested product. The network is centered on the only experimentally demonstrated small-molecule framework: surface formose chemistry supplies aldotetroses from formaldehyde, HCN/NH3 concentration supplies the contested amidine formamidine, tetrose-amidine annulation yields the decisive low-yield imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and HCN/NH3 then close the route by Strecker chemistry. Imidazole-4-glycol and imidazole-4-ethanol are included as experimentally observed side branches and speculative salvage reservoirs. The 10 listed pathways are route variants within one connected network, not 10 equally validated syntheses. Following the supplied evidence, hydrothermal, cyanosulfidic, meteoritic, trace HCN-polymer, spark-discharge, and semi-biotic PRPP/ATP proposals are not promoted to productive edges here because they lack a comparably resolved small-molecule reaction sequence.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrocyanic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "glyceraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "erythrose", "common_name": "erythrose", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "threose", "common_name": "threose", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CH4N2", "iupac_name": "methanimidamide", "common_name": "formamidine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal", "common_name": "imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethane-1,2-diol", "common_name": "imidazole-4-glycol", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethan-1-ol", "common_name": "imidazole-4-ethanol", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H8N4", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "histidine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "histidine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Formose initiation: formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Alkaline wet-dry formose regime in evaporitic pools or tidal flats; anoxic surface; low steady-state glycolaldehyde expected"}, "mechanism": "Net formaldehyde dimerization via enediol/aldol chemistry in the formose network.", "reasoning": "The histidine papers assume a formose origin for the C4 sugar pool; this compressed edge represents the initiation of that upstream chemistry. It is not a histidine-specific experiment in the supplied report, and the main caveat is the low, unstable sugar inventory emphasized by later critiques (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Formose homologation: glycolaldehyde to glyceraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Alkaline wet-dry formose regime; compressed representation of the triose-forming segment of the formose network"}, "mechanism": "Aldol homologation followed by enolization and isomerization within the formose network.", "reasoning": "This step is included because the Shen route requires upstream conversion of formaldehyde to a C4 sugar precursor. As with rxn_001, it is a compressed, non-histidine-specific upstream edge whose relevance is limited by the poor prebiotic accumulation of higher sugars noted in the critique literature (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Formose homologation: glyceraldehyde to aldotetroses", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Alkaline surface pool; aldotetrose mixture expected; erythrose and threose remain minor and unstable"}, "mechanism": "Further aldol homologation gives an aldotetrose mixture, here represented by erythrose and threose epimers.", "reasoning": "This step generates the C4 aldoses needed for imidazole side-chain construction. Shen et al. used erythrose as the key sugar, while later critiques stressed that erythrose and threose are minor, unstable formose products and therefore difficult to accumulate prebiotically (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Amidine supply: HCN and ammonia to formamidine", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "HCN/NH3-rich evaporitic concentration; transient amidine pool; rapid hydrolysis competes"}, "mechanism": "Net ammonolysis and addition to cyanide-derived imidoyl species giving methanimidamide.", "reasoning": "Formamidine is the required amidine feedstock in the Shen-Oro route, but the supplied literature does not show a robust geochemical accumulation route at the needed concentration. Later critiques emphasize that about 0.3 M formamidine is hard to justify because formamidine hydrolyzes rapidly; this edge is therefore necessary for the network but strongly contested geochemically (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Erythrose-formamidine annulation to imidazole aldehyde/glycol pair", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Concentrated aqueous pool; exact pH and time from the original report were not recovered; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Carbonyl condensation of the aldotetrose with formamidine, Amadori-type rearrangement, and cyclodehydration to the imidazole ring.", "reasoning": "This is the central experimentally reported bottleneck for abiotic histidine chemistry. Shen, Yang, Miller and Oro obtained imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (up to 1.6% relative to erythrose) together with imidazole-4-glycol from erythrose plus formamidine in water, without a mineral catalyst; uncatalyzed imidazole-forming chemistry is also consistent with broader imidazole condensations (Shen et al., 1987; Oro et al., 1984). Its prebiotic significance is heavily challenged because both erythrose supply and formamidine persistence are poor (Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Threose-formamidine annulation to imidazole aldehyde/glycol pair", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Concentrated aqueous pool; evidence-guided extrapolation from the erythrose experiment; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Same tetrose-formamidine annulation manifold as rxn_005, with loss of tetrose stereochemical information during ring closure.", "reasoning": "The provided experiments centered on erythrose, not isolated threose. This edge is included as an evidence-guided extrapolation because Vazquez-Salazar et al. note that the stereocenters of the tetrose are lost during annulation and therefore any C4 aldose should in principle serve; the reaction is chemically plausible but remains unverified for threose specifically (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia annulation to reduced imidazole pair", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Aqueous erythrose/H2CO/NH3 mixture; exact pH and time from the original report were not recovered; reduced side-chain branch"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-mediated carbonyl condensation and imidazole ring closure produce reduced C2 side-chain imidazole derivatives.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. reported that erythrose, formaldehyde and ammonia give imidazole-4-ethanol (5.4%) and imidazole-4-glycol (6.8%). This branch is experimentally supported as a side-product source, but it is not by itself a histidine route because conversion of these reduced products to the required aldehyde was only proposed, not demonstrated (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Speculative salvage: imidazole-4-glycol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "UV-exposed drying film or geothermal cycling; net dehydration/oxidation; explicitly undemonstrated"}, "mechanism": "Net redox and rearrangement of the hydroxy-substituted C2 side chain to the aldehyde.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. argued that imidazole-4-glycol could, in principle, be converted by other prebiotic reactions to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, but no such transformation was shown in the supplied literature. This node is therefore explicitly speculative and encodes the open redox bottleneck highlighted by later critiques (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Speculative salvage: imidazole-4-ethanol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "UV-exposed drying film or geothermal cycling; net oxidation step; explicitly undemonstrated"}, "mechanism": "Net oxidation of the primary alcohol side chain to the aldehyde during UV and thermal cycling.", "reasoning": "The 1987 study proposed, but did not demonstrate, that imidazole-4-ethanol could be prebiotically oxidized to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde. This edge is thus speculative, included only because the reduced alcohol was experimentally observed and the literature explicitly identifies conversion to the aldehyde as the missing step (Shen et al., 1987; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Strecker capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "HCN/NH3-rich pool; mechanistic decomposition of the overall 1990 one-pot result"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine or iminium with the aldehyde, cyanide adds, and the aminonitrile is trapped.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. 1990 demonstrated the overall conversion of the unpurified imidazole-4-acetaldehyde-containing mixture to histidine after addition of HCN and ammonia. The aminonitrile itself was not isolated in the supplied sources, so this step is a mechanistic decomposition of the validated overall transformation, supported by general Strecker chemistry and by pairing of histidine with imidazole-4-acetaldehyde in Strecker models (Shen et al., 1990; Cobb et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Prolonged aqueous heating or geothermal cycling in prebiotic pools"}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis to the amino acid carboxylate.", "reasoning": "This step is also mechanistically inferred rather than individually reported for histidine in the supplied literature. It is nevertheless the standard completion of a Strecker sequence; the main unresolved issue is selectivity because competing cyanohydrin and hydroxy-acid channels were not quantified for the imidazole aldehyde system (Shen et al., 1990; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018; Cobb et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "One-pot Strecker-hydrolysis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "HCN and NH3 added directly to the unpurified aldehyde-forming mixture; exact pH and time were not recovered from the supplied report"}, "mechanism": "Net Strecker sequence from aldehyde to aminonitrile followed by in situ hydrolysis in the same reaction system.", "reasoning": "This is the only experimentally demonstrated small-molecule closure to histidine in the supplied literature. Shen, Yang, Miller and Oro identified histidine by LC, HPLC, thermospray LC-MS, and tandem MS after adding HCN and ammonia to the imidazole-4-acetaldehyde-forming mixture, with an overall yield of about 3.5% relative to erythrose. The chemistry is sound, but its prebiotic status remains contested because the route depends on unstable tetroses and concentrated formamidine, and no independent modern isotope-labeled replication is cited in the provided review (Shen et al., 1990; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Evidence-anchored erythrose one-pot route", "description": "Formaldehyde-derived erythrose and HCN/NH3-derived formamidine converge at the experimentally anchored annulation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, followed by the one-pot Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Erythrose direct stepwise Strecker route", "description": "Same erythrose annulation entry as P1, but the terminal one-pot closure is decomposed into aminonitrile formation and later hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Erythrose glycol-salvage one-pot route", "description": "Uses the experimentally observed imidazole-4-glycol coproduct from the erythrose/formamidine reaction as a speculative reservoir that is converted to the aldehyde before one-pot Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_010", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Erythrose glycol-salvage stepwise route", "description": "Same as P3 but with explicit aminonitrile formation and later hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_010", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Erythrose ethanol-salvage one-pot route", "description": "Starts from the experimentally observed erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia branch to imidazole-4-ethanol, then invokes a speculative oxidation to the aldehyde before one-pot Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Erythrose ethanol-salvage stepwise route", "description": "Alcohol-reservoir salvage variant of P5 with explicit aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Erythrose glycol-reservoir route from the H2CO/NH3 branch", "description": "Uses the glycol coproduct generated in the erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia branch and converts it speculatively to the aldehyde before one-pot Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_010", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Threose extrapolated one-pot route", "description": "Evidence-guided extrapolation in which the aldotetrose epimer threose replaces erythrose in the formamidine annulation; final closure uses the experimentally demonstrated one-pot Strecker chemistry.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Threose extrapolated stepwise route", "description": "Threose-based extrapolation of the Shen pathway with explicit aminonitrile and hydrolysis substeps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Threose glycol-salvage stepwise route", "description": "Threose-based pathway that passes through the coproduct imidazole-4-glycol before speculative salvage to the aldehyde and stepwise Strecker completion.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_012"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_010", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_013", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 658057.3449134