ons fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.68,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.43,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.6,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.59,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.77,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.64,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.64,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the best-aligned route with the Shen et al. small-molecule literature: tetrose formation, contested formamidine supply, erythrose-formamidine annulation, then one-pot Strecker/hydrolysis to histidine. The route is coherent, but rxn_005 violates conservation as written and should either be split into separate branches (erythrose + formamidine -> mol_009 + 3 H2O; erythrose + formamidine -> mol_010 + 2 H2O) or rewritten with explicit coefficients and H2O byproduct. rxn_012 is also mis-specified; the balanced net one-pot chemistry is better represented as mol_009 + mol_002 + H2O -> mol_013 with NH3 as a condition/catalyst, or else as corrected rxn_010 + rxn_011. rxn_004 compresses the unresolved accumulation of concentrated formamidine from HCN/NH3, which the literature identifies as a major bottleneck.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Split rxn_005 into separate product-forming reactions or add explicit coefficients and H2O byproduct.\",\n        \"Rewrite rxn_012 as mol_009 + mol_002 + H2O -> mol_013 with NH3 as a condition, or replace it with corrected rxn_010 + rxn_011.\",\n        \"Specify a surface setting and concentration mechanism that could co-accumulate tetroses and formamidine despite formose instability and amidine hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\