um, cyanide addition, and hydrolysis of the resulting aminonitrile to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the key experimentally demonstrated terminal edge. Shen et al. added HCN and ammonia to the unpurified imidazole-4-acetaldehyde mixture and detected histidine at 3.5% relative to erythrose. It is written here as the balanced net transformation because ammonia participates mechanistically but cancels overall. The result remains contested because the precursor supply is weak and there has been no independent modern isotope-labeled replication in the supplied literature (Shen et al., 1990; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2016; Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Classical HCN/NH3 Strecker conditions in anoxic aqueous solution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde imine formation with ammonia followed by cyanide addition gives the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction makes explicit the standard Strecker intermediate that is mechanistically implicit in rxn_010. The specific histidine aminonitrile was not isolated in the supplied papers, so this node is inferred, but imidazole-4-acetaldehyde is the accepted aldehyde precursor for Strecker-style histidine formation in both the Shen framework and parent-body modeling (Shen et al., 1990; Cobb et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"