e could in principle work, but the supplied literature does not directly quantify the threose version. It is therefore chemically reasonable but weaker than the erythrose edge (Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Threose-formamidine annulation to imidazole-4-glycol reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Surface concentration regime analogous to rxn_004; anoxic."}, "mechanism": "Threose follows the same annulation manifold but is trapped as the glycol or hydrated aldehyde reservoir.", "reasoning": "This is the speculative threose analogue of the erythrose glycol branch. It is included because the supplied critique explicitly notes that the tetrose stereocenters need not survive into the imidazole product, but no direct prebiotic experimental confirmation for this specific threose edge was reported (Vazquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Erythrose plus formaldehyde plus ammonia to imidazole-4-ethanol", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Erythrose plus added formaldehyde and ammonia variant of the Shen chemistry; anoxic surface pool."}, "mechanism": "Carbonyl-ammonia condensation and cyclization generate the imidazole ring, followed by net reduction to the alcohol side chain.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. reported imidazole-4-ethanol in up to 5.4% yield from an erythrose plus formaldehyde plus ammonia system. This branch is retained as a side reservoir because conversion of the alcohol to the aldehyde required for histidine was suggested but not demonstrated under prebiotic conditions. The same paper also discussed related glycol production,