t coefficients and H2O byproduct. rxn_012 is also mis-specified; the balanced net one-pot chemistry is better represented as mol_009 + mol_002 + H2O -> mol_013 with NH3 as a condition/catalyst, or else as corrected rxn_010 + rxn_011. rxn_004 compresses the unresolved accumulation of concentrated formamidine from HCN/NH3, which the literature identifies as a major bottleneck.', 'Mechanistically this is clearer than P1 because it exposes the histidine aminonitrile intermediate, and corrected rxn_010 is standard Strecker chemistry. However, rxn_005 remains unbalanced, rxn_010 needs H2O as product, and rxn_011 is conservation-violating as written; it should be mol_012 + 2 H2O -> mol_013 + NH3. The jump from Surface to Biochemical is not justified in a prebiotic network unless a semi-biotic branch is explicitly intended. The overall logic matches the literature, but the submitted reaction specification does not.', 'This is a reasonable low-confidence reservoir variant because mol_010 is reported in the Shen annulation chemistry. But rxn_008 is undemonstrated and only reasonably plausible if mol_010 is defined as the aldehyde hydrate; as written it needs H2O as product. rxn_005 and rxn_012 retain the same stoichiometric problems as in P1. The literature treats imidazole-4-glycol as a side branch or sink, not an established productive intermediate en route to histidine.', 'This stepwise glycol-salvage route is weaker than P2 because it adds the undemonstrated rescue of mol_010 to mol_009 before the already miswritten hydrolysis step. rxn_008 needs H2O as product if mol_010 is the hydrate; rxn_010 needs H2O as product; rxn_011 requires water as reactant and NH3 release. The route is coherent on paper, but the key salvage chemistry remains speculative in the literature.', 'This is a very weak route. The literature does report imidazole-4-ethanol from erythrose + formaldehyde + ammonia, but rxn_007 is impossible as written: one NH3 cannot supply the two nitrogens present in either imidazole product, and the mass mismatch is not fixed by a single common small species. rxn_009 also needs explicit redox bookkeeping, at minimum H2 as product or a specified oxidant/redox partner, and it has no direct prebiotic demonstration in this context. Even before the mis-specified one-pot terminal step, this branch is better treated as a speculative side sink than as a productive histidine pathway.', 'This route is not stronger than P5 and is environmentally worse because it also invokes the unjustified Biochemical hydrolysis step. rxn_007 is not conservation-correct and undercounts nitrogen, rxn_009 is a speculative oxidation/dehydrogenation, rxn_010 needs H2O as product, and rxn_011 should be mol_012 + 2 H2O -> mol_013 + NH3. As submitted, it is not a defensible prebiotic pathway to histidine.', 'As a reservoir concept this is somewhat more plausible than the ethanol-salvage routes, because mol_010 may be closer to the aldehyde oxidation state, but the entry step rxn_007 is still badly mis-specified and rxn_008 is undemonstrated. The pathway also depends on the same incorrect one-pot bookkeeping in rxn_012. Literature support exists for mol_010 as a side product, not for a validated mol_010 -> mol_009 -> histidine reservoir route.', 'This is a reasonable extrapolation from the literature argument that C4-aldose stereochemistry is lost in the annulation, so threose could feed the same imidazole aldehyde chemistry. Still, rxn_006 has the same stoichiometric defect as rxn_005 and should include water and preferably be split into separate product branches. The final one-pot rxn_012 is again mis-specified. This path is chemically less evidenced than the erythrose route but not inherently implausible after correction.', 'This stepwise threose route is coherent as an extrapolated analogue of the erythrose path, and corrected rxn_010 is standard Strecker chemistry. But the pathway still depends on unbalanced rxn_006 and a miswritten rxn_011, and the Surface -> Biochemical transition is not justified. It should be treated only as a low-weight analogue of the erythrose route, not as an equally validated pathway.', 'This is the most speculative threose-based route because it combines an extrapolated annulation with an undemonstrated glycol salvage and the mis-specified hydrolysis step. rxn_006 needs corrected stoichiometry, rxn_008 needs H2O as product and mechanistic justification, rxn_010 needs H2O as product, and rxn_011 needs water reactant plus NH3 release. It is coherent as a hypothesis tree, but not yet a defensible productive pathway.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.75\n- Feedback: Connected, literature-aware network with good validity and strong alignment to the supplied histidine research. It uses the only experimentally grounded small-molecule framework (C4 aldose + formamidine -> imidazole-4-acetaldehyde -> Strecker/hydrolysis) and appropriately keeps histidine low-confidence and low-flux; the decision not to expand hydrothermal, cyanosulfidic, spark, or meteoritic routes is consistent with the literature. Validity is good because all nodes are connected and RDKit found 0 UNBALANCED reactions, but 7 reactions need explicit stoichiometric correction: rxn_001 should be split or written as 8 CH2O -> erythrose + threose; rxn_003 and rxn_005 need 3 H2O; rxn_004 and rxn_006 need 2 H2O; rxn_007 needs 2 NH3 and 4 H2O; rxn_012 needs 2 H2O. The core weakness is environmental and availability, not the final Strecker chemistry: tetroses are minor unstable formose products, formamidine accumulation is contested because of hydrolysis, and the generic Surface label needs more explicit alkaline/wet-dry concentration conditions. Auxiliary salvage edges are under-supported, especially rxn_009; rxn_008 is also not demonstrated. rxn_010 is acceptable as a net one-pot transformation, but NH3 should be stated as present in the pot even if it cancels from net stoichiometry. Overall this is an acceptable but not approved network: the core route is chemically plausible and literature-backed, yet the precursor supply problem and speculative reservoir-to-aldehyde salvage steps keep confidence below approval threshold. A separate semi-biotic IGP-like hypothesis would improve completeness but is not required for core validity.\n- Issues: ['Strongest route and closest to the Shen-Yang-Miller-Oro literature. rxn_003 and the overall one-pot outcome behind rxn_010 are experimentally grounded, but the upstream supply problem remains severe: rxn_001 abstracts a low-selectivity formose process and should be split or written as 8 CH2O -> erythrose + threose; rxn_002 assumes surface accumulation of labile formamidine; rxn_003 should be written with 3 H2O. rxn_010 is acceptable as a net step, but NH3 should be stated as present in the pot even if it cancels from net stoichiometry.', 'Mechanistically clearer than P1 because Strecker capture and hydrolysis are shown explicitly. However, the literature directly demonstrates the one-pot histidine outcome more strongly than isolated histidine aminonitrile, and the same concentration objections for rxn_001-rxn_003 remain. rxn_003 needs 3 H2O, and rxn_012 should be written as aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3.', 'Reasonable as a reservoir hypothesis because imidazole-4-glycol is reported, but the productive salvage edge is weak. rxn_004 is literature-aligned as a coproduct, yet rxn_008 is not demonstrated in the supplied literature and should not be treated as a robust dehydration shortcut. Stoichiometrically, rxn_004 needs 2 H2O; rxn_010 again needs NH3 stated in the pot.', 'This pathway is chemically coherent only if the speculative glycol-to-aldehyde salvage is accepted. The literature supports formation of mol_010 much better than its conversion by rxn_008, so the bottleneck is shifted rather than solved. rxn_004 needs 2 H2O, and rxn_012 needs 2 H2O for hydrolysis.', 'Plausible extrapolation of the core Shen framework to threose. The literature indicates that C4 stereochemistry is lost and any C4 aldose may work, but direct support is still weaker than for erythrose. rxn_005 should be written with 3 H2O, and the same formose/formamidine availability concerns apply.', 'Acceptable mechanistic variant of the threose route, but weaker than the erythrose analogue and still limited by tetrose and formamidine supply. rxn_005 needs 3 H2O, while rxn_012 needs 2 H2O. The explicit aminonitrile sequence is chemically sound but less directly evidenced than the reported one-pot transformation.', 'This is a doubly weakened version of the core route: the threose annulation is less directly supported, and the glycol salvage rxn_008 is not experimentally established. rxn_006 needs 2 H2O, and rxn_010 should note NH3 in the pot.', 'Chemically possible on paper, but not well grounded experimentally. The critical weakness is still rxn_008, with additional uncertainty from the threose extrapolation. rxn_006 should include 2 H2O and rxn_012 should include 2 H2O.', 'The literature supports formation of imidazole-4-ethanol by rxn_007, but not its productive return to the aldehyde under the generic surface conditions given. rxn_009 is the weakest edge in the whole network: dehydrogenation of a primary alcohol to an aldehyde with free H2 release needs a specified oxidant, photoredox process, or catalytic mineral/metal surface. Also correct rxn_007 to use 2 NH3 and produce 4 H2O.', 'Explicit Strecker chemistry cannot rescue the core weakness here, which is the unsupported alcohol-to-aldehyde salvage. rxn_009 remains chemically underjustified in the stated environment, rxn_007 needs 2 NH3 and 4 H2O, and rxn_012 needs 2 H2O.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Histidine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Use the only experimentally grounded prebiotic framework for histidine: formose-derived C4 aldoses and HCN/NH3-derived formamidine in surface evaporitic pools. The network centers the low-yield erythrose/formamidine annulation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, then reaches histidine either by the experimentally observed one-pot HCN/NH3 conversion or by the explicit Strecker aminonitrile plus hydrolysis sequence. Threose analogues and imidazole-4-glycol salvage are included as low-weight extrapolations, so the 10 pathways are variants of one contested chemistry rather than 10 independent validated syntheses. Unsupported hydrothermal, meteoritic, spark, and cyanosulfidic routes are not encoded as productive histidine-forming edges.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanimidamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formamidine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"erythro-2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"erythrose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"threo-2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethane-1,1-diol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-5(6(10)11)1-4-2-8-3-9-4/h2-3,5H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose tetrose branch from formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline wet-dry formose chemistry in evaporitic pools; low tetrose selectivity; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-promoted aldolization and isomerization of formaldehyde generate low-abundance tetroses.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This net step supplies the C4 aldoses required by the Shen histidine framework. Shen et al. (1987) presupposed erythrose from formaldehyde/formose chemistry, but V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2016) and V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) emphasize that erythrose and threose are minor, unstable formose products, so this is a major bottleneck and is represented as low-flux rather than robust.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-ammonia amidination to formamidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"evaporative concentration in HCN/NH3-rich pools; mildly basic; amidine hydrolysis competes strongly\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia addition to cyanide-derived carbon followed by tautomerization gives the amidine pool.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formamidine is the amidine used in the only experimental histidine synthesis from small molecules (Shen et al., 1987; Shen et al., 1990). The chemistry is simple enough to include, but its environmental accumulation is heavily contested because the literature reports the need for high formamidine concentration and rapid hydrolysis undermines persistence (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2016; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine annulation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"concentrated aqueous pool or drying film; exact original Shen conditions are incompletely available in the supplied report\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of the tetrose with formamidine, followed by an Amadori-type rearrangement, imidazole ring closure, and dehydration, yields the aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the central experimentally supported bottleneck. Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-acetaldehyde from erythrose plus formamidine at up to about 1.6% based on erythrose. Later critiques argue that the chemistry is real but prebiotically weak because simultaneous accumulation of erythrose and formamidine is unlikely (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2016; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine annulation to imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"same low-selectivity annulation regime as rxn_003; side-product branch\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Parallel annulation and hydration chemistry diverts the tetrose/amidine system into a more oxygenated imidazole side product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-glycol at up to about 6.8% based on erythrose. The literature treats this as a coproduct or sink, not as a validated productive intermediate to histidine. It is included here conservatively as a reservoir branch, with downstream rescue kept explicitly low confidence (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-formamidine annulation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"concentrated aqueous pool or drying film; direct threose-specific histidine data are unavailable\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same aldose-amidine annulation sequence is extrapolated to the threo tetrose isomer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This edge is speculative but chemically motivated. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) note that the stereochemical information in the C4 aldose is lost during annulation, so any C4 aldose could in principle feed the same imidazole aldehyde. No direct threose-specific histidine experiment was reported by Shen et al., so this branch is assigned low weight.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-formamidine annulation to imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"same extrapolated threose annulation regime as rxn_005; side-product branch\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Parallel annulation and hydration chemistry from threose generates the glycol reservoir branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a second speculative threose analogue. It follows from the same stereochemistry-erasing argument discussed by V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018), but no direct report shows threose producing this exact imidazole glycol. It is included only as a low-confidence extension of the Shen framework.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycol reservoir collapse to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"drying or concentration-assisted aldehyde-hydrate collapse; highly speculative salvage edge\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"If the glycol behaves as or equilibrates with an aldehyde hydrate, dehydration returns the free aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is explicitly speculative. Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-glycol, but no productive conversion back to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde was shown in the histidine system. The edge is included only by treating the reported glycol as a hydrate-like reservoir that can collapse to the aldehyde; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) specifically note that such extra transformations were not demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN- and NH3-rich aqueous pool; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The aldehyde forms an imine or iminium with ammonia, cyanide adds, and the histidine aminonitrile is produced with water loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the expected mechanistic Strecker step from the Shen system. Shen et al. (1990) did not isolate the aminonitrile but obtained histidine after adding HCN and NH3 to the aldehyde-containing mixture, implying this intermediate. The pairing of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde with histidine in Strecker logic is also used in parent-body modeling (Cobb et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"aqueous aging or mild heating; ammonia released on nitrile hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential hydration of the nitrile to amide and then acid completes the amino acid skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This intermediate-level step was not independently isolated in the supplied histidine literature, so it is less directly evidenced than the net one-pot transformation. It is nevertheless the standard completion of Strecker chemistry and is mechanistically required if the Shen et al. (1990) product arose through the conventional aminonitrile pathway (Shen et al., 1990; Cobb et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Net one-pot conversion of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN and NH3 added to the unpurified annulation mixture; NH3 present in the pot but net-neutral in the balanced overall step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net Strecker capture followed by aminonitrile hydrolysis gives histidine without isolating the intermediates.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the experimentally supported terminal net step. Shen et al. (1990) converted the unpurified imidazole-4-acetaldehyde mixture to histidine by adding HCN and ammonia, identifying histidine at about 3.5% relative to erythrose. Because the aminonitrile hydrolysis returns ammonia, NH3 is a condition of the pot even though it cancels from the net stoichiometry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Net one-pot erythrose-to-histidine sequence\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"single pooled surface setting with evaporative concentration; NH3 present in the pot but omitted stoichiometrically because it is net-neutral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A balanced net compression of annulation, Strecker capture, and hydrolysis in the erythrose branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This node compresses the experimentally reported erythrose route into a single balanced one-pot transformation. Shen et al. (1990) effectively executed this sequence by forming the imidazole precursor mixture first and then adding HCN and ammonia without isolating intermediates. The chemistry is literature-guided but remains severely concentration-limited and prebiotically contested because of the tetrose and formamidine supply problem (Shen et al., 1990; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2016; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Net one-pot threose-to-histidine sequence\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"single pooled surface setting with evaporative concentration; NH3 present in the pot but omitted stoichiometrically because it is net-neutral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A speculative net compression of annulation, Strecker capture, and hydrolysis in the threose branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a low-weight analogue of rxn_011. It is included because later discussion of the Shen chemistry argues that C4-aldose stereochemistry is erased during annulation, so threose could in principle substitute for erythrose. No direct threose-to-histidine experiment has been reported, so this edge remains explicitly speculative (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018; Shen et al., 1990).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Core erythrose annulation plus net terminal conversion\",\n      \"description\": \"Best-supported explicit route: formose-derived erythrose, contested formamidine supply, experimentally reported aldehyde formation, then the experimentally supported net terminal HCN/NH3 conversion.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Core erythrose annulation with explicit aminonitrile\",\n      \"description\": \"Mechanistic expansion of the erythrose route that exposes the histidine aminonitrile intermediate rather than collapsing the terminal chemistry into one net step.\",\n      \"